WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.190
- Actually don't sit down, I
wanna start with something.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:07.400
Before we get going here, I
just need like a boxing tip.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.380
Like I don't intend to
get into a lot of fights.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.570
I don't get in a lot of
fights in my everyday life...

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.560
- Just in case.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.440
- But just in case.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.890
I know you've been in the
ring with Floyd Mayweather.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.670
Arguably the greatest boxer of all time,

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.320
so where should I put my hands?

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.460
- So I gotta say, first,

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.660
you should be asking my
brother this question.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.840
- Okay, okay.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.340
- He's undefeated, and I am the opposite.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.670
- Okay, but you did go eight
rounds with Floyd, I believe.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:30.708
- Yeah, okay, okay, okay, okay,

00:00:30.708 --> 00:00:32.974
so I'm gonna go ahead, take
my watch off for this one.

00:00:32.974 --> 00:00:35.240
- No, you don't have
to take the watch off,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:36.504
what are we doing here?

00:00:36.504 --> 00:00:37.337
- Just put your hand like this.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.190
- Which one?

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.023
- This one.

00:00:39.023 --> 00:00:40.063
- All right.

00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:40.896
- So I'm a little bit of
an unorthodox fighter,

00:00:40.896 --> 00:00:41.926
and I fight with my hands down.

00:00:41.926 --> 00:00:42.759
- I hear that, okay.

00:00:42.759 --> 00:00:45.210
- And my jab is not your
typical jab from up here.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:46.043
- Okay.

00:00:46.043 --> 00:00:48.326
- I do like a snappy jab,
kinda like a backhand.

00:00:48.326 --> 00:00:49.958
- Geez, geez, geez.

00:00:49.958 --> 00:00:51.230
- It's like a backhand.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.540
- Do you know about my
healthcare plan? Take it easy.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.710
- No, no, it's all good,

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.380
now you gotta try though, obviously.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.213
- So like a jab.

00:00:57.213 --> 00:00:59.370
- Imagine like a backhand as a jab.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.203
- Okay.

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:02.250
- A little more, a little more.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.450
- Little more like this?

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:05.203
Oh, back of the hand.

00:01:05.203 --> 00:01:06.760
- You know what? Let's do the panel.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.290
- Okay, all right, all right,
good enough, good enough.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.427
Okay, stick to writing columns,

00:01:10.427 --> 00:01:12.200
is what you're saying, right?

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.934
- No, it's good, it's good.

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:14.960
I saw the tenacity in your eyes.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.170
- All right, listen, if I
ever win a fight, you get 10%.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:19.003
How's does that sound?

00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:19.960
- Thank you, I appreciate that.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.120
- So I wanna get to the boxing stuff,

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.070
but this is the "Wall
Street Journal", Logan.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.920
So we probably should
start with some business.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.290
- [Logan] Cool.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.210
- You've been at this,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.330
I don't know how old you feel,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.170
but you've been at this
for a long time now.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.500
I was looking at the record,
it's almost 10 years for you

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.500
in the business of social media.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.333
- Yeah.

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.430
- You're only 27.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:40.263
- Yeah.

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:41.568
- But do you kind of feel
like you've been around

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:42.880
for a while in the business?

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.641
- I do, I do, I realized
when young creators

00:01:46.641 --> 00:01:49.770
would start coming up to
me, that I'm now the vet.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.150
And I had that moment with
the creators that inspired me.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.900
And I look around at the landscape,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.830
and I do have a pretty keen
understanding of social,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.510
and that only comes with time.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.443
So I do have to admit, yeah,

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.731
I'm knowledged in this space
because of my experience.

00:02:08.731 --> 00:02:10.580
Like, I am a vet.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.460
- Social media feels like
it should be measured

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.960
in dog years, I mean,
it feels like 10 years

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.017
should be 80 years, or
something like that.

00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:19.008
- Well because it's always changing.

00:02:19.008 --> 00:02:20.520
There's always a new platform,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.530
and each platform has
a different language.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.370
Just like code on a website,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.403
HTML5, DOS, whatever it is,

00:02:28.403 --> 00:02:31.130
it's a language, and social's the same.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.960
I just happened to get good at speaking

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.390
the different languages,
native to each platform.

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.060
- Now, if you could go back and
visit Logan Paul circa 2012,

00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:46.320
2013, we're talking about
still at Ohio University guy,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.110
still starting out with Vine.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.884
And you could give
yourself a couple of pieces

00:02:49.884 --> 00:02:51.680
of advice for the next decade.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.483
What would you say to that guy?

00:02:54.992 --> 00:02:57.520
- I would've told him to go for it.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.600
It's the one thing I didn't think I did

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.823
until I had a case study
that proved to be working.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.260
When I dropped out of college,

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.390
I finished my first year with a 4.0,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.714
it was a hard decision, because
I am a bit of an academic,

00:03:13.714 --> 00:03:15.520
if you will, I love...

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:17.210
- What were you headed toward?

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.940
Like had none of this happened,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:19.970
what career do you think
you would've pursued.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.000
- I was going to be an
industrial and systems engineer.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.640
- Okay, I don't even know what that is.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.590
- They focus on efficiency in factories.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:27.423
- Fantastic.

00:03:27.423 --> 00:03:30.380
- Yeah. So, it was creative though.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.230
You know, my goal, at that point in time,

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:37.500
was to invent a product that
would revolutionize the world.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.950
And it turns out media allows me

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.990
to exercise that creative
part of my brain.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.490
And it's a lot more fun.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:47.500
- Yeah, I bet.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.730
I mean, there was no blueprint for this.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.900
There was no book you
could check out of Amazon,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.070
there was no, "How to
be a social media star".

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.620
You know, it meant
successes, it meant mistakes.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.155
You've had some high-profile mistakes too.

00:03:58.155 --> 00:04:00.480
What did you learn along the way?

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.310
What do you think were
the most critical lessons?

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.353
- Yeah, you know, you mentioned blueprint.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.270
I think anyone paving the way,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.890
kind of making their own blueprint,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.980
understands the difficulties
of what goes into it.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:18.220
Because you know, there's
no path to follow,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:21.040
you're paving the path, but
it also leads to sometimes

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.023
the most rewarding results.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.900
I've had a career full of ups and down,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.157
successes and failures,
but I take everything,

00:04:31.157 --> 00:04:32.100
everything that I do,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.730
and I apply it to the
next thing that I do.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.430
The learnings, and understandings,

00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:41.260
and I've gotten good at acknowledging

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:42.990
and accepting my truth,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.330
and my reality, and then
using it to move forward.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.823
And at this point, I've done a lot.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.150
I joke with my friends
like, I know too much.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:57.228
I know too much, and I feel like I can use

00:04:57.228 --> 00:05:01.600
what I've learned to continue
creating that blueprint.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.280
- The flip side of all of this,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.050
the flip side of internet fame,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.100
or any kind of fame really,

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:06.933
is that there's gonna be a certain number

00:05:06.933 --> 00:05:08.737
of people who are gonna love you,

00:05:08.737 --> 00:05:10.450
and there's always gonna
be a certain amount number

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:11.620
of people who dislike you.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.110
Maybe even hate you, and
that's the way it works.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.540
And anger's a very potent
thing on the internet.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.060
- Sure, sure.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.510
- Does hate roll off of you at this point,

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.920
or does it stick to you
and bother you sometimes?

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.550
- No, hate rolls off of me.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.102
I have empathy for anyone
who has legitimate hatred

00:05:28.102 --> 00:05:31.497
in their heart, it feels burdening to me.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:35.525
And I simply don't have time

00:05:35.525 --> 00:05:39.420
to focus negative energy anywhere.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.540
And I've barreled through it all.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.080
And I've learned that
truly if you don't give up,

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:46.900
you're gonna be a tough person to stop.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:51.900
And social media's full of
haters, naysayers, doubters.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.840
And to me it's all just
noise, because it really is,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.510
it's a big world, and
everyone has an opinion.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.720
But if you know who you are,

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.553
and you know what you're up to

00:05:59.553 --> 00:06:00.700
and how you're gonna get there,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.000
none of the other stuff matters.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.890
- I mean, a word that gets
brought up all the time,

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.530
to the point that it's almost
meaningless, is authenticity.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.520
And you once said like,
"This brand I've built,

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.130
you either love me or hate
me for exactly who I am.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.080
There's no smoke, no mirrors."

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.030
You know, can you judge, when
you're looking at other people

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:19.210
who are coming up, when someone's
being genuinely authentic,

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.250
versus like sort of fake-authentic?

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.820
- Sure, sure, of course.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.990
And not only just me,
I think the internet,

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:28.780
and people in general, are becoming more

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.730
and more aware of who is real.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.710
And I truly do think authenticity will be

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:38.780
the key for creators,
celebrities, influencers,

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:43.780
in this next decade
because everything we see

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.140
has a level of superficiality
to it, or fickleness,

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.310
or you don't know if it's real or fake.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.610
Even just like more macro,
like deep-fakes now,

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:56.500
like in the next 10 years or so,

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:57.880
you're not gonna know who's saying what,

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.290
because you're able to
recreate someone's face

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:02.283
and have them speak as if they're them.

00:07:03.358 --> 00:07:04.500
- But they're probably
on the flip side of that,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.607
there are probably people who argue like,

00:07:05.607 --> 00:07:06.868
"Well, what gives me the
need for paying influencers,

00:07:06.868 --> 00:07:09.190
when we can just create them?"

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.480
Do you think that'll always
be a fundamental need

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:13.500
for actual humans to do this stuff?

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.867
- I don't know, I don't
like to speak in absolutes.

00:07:16.867 --> 00:07:18.700
So, I wouldn't say "always".

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.080
But my knee-jerk reaction would be yes.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:25.438
I think like genuine,
authentic human connection,

00:07:25.438 --> 00:07:30.146
the ability to navigate
and garner attention,

00:07:30.146 --> 00:07:35.146
comes with a human, a
homosapien sapien, so yeah.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:38.250
- You have a famous younger brother.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.130
- He is famous.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.360
- I don't know if you know that.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.382
You mentioned him at the top,

00:07:42.382 --> 00:07:44.870
I believe you live across
the street from each other?

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.130
- Yeah, yep.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:47.650
- Are you competitive with each other,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:49.070
or is this a real partnership?

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.400
- No, not anymore.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.365
There was a time, this
is embarrassing to say,

00:07:54.365 --> 00:07:56.860
but my most viewed YouTube video

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:59.170
is a diss track against my brother.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:04.020
So yeah, there was a time where
we were definitely enemies.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.650
And then we realized,
this is a weird journey.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:12.070
Personally. I have one
brother, whom I love dearly,

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:14.630
and he's always gonna have
my back no matter what.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:19.630
And in this interesting, eccentric,

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:23.770
difficult to navigate life
that we happen to end up in,

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.270
there's not another person I can turn to

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:29.364
that truly understands
what's happening and why.

00:08:29.364 --> 00:08:32.620
We grew up in Ohio together in the woods.

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.370
Locked out of the house
for 13 hours a day,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.270
because our dad made us play
outside and do manual labor.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.580
And then all of a sudden, I'm
fighting Floyd Mayweather,

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.724
and Jake is one of the highest
paid athletes in the world.

00:08:45.724 --> 00:08:47.780
How does that happen?

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:48.620
- It's surreal.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:50.500
I mean, I don't know
the last time you guys

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.800
got in a car together, just
alone and drove somewhere,

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.990
but what is that conversation
like, just the two of you?

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.550
- They're the best
conversations I have in my life,

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.097
because we spend time
apart from each other.

00:09:01.097 --> 00:09:04.849
And he has just as much passion for life,

00:09:04.849 --> 00:09:08.100
and fervor, as I do.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:13.060
And so we learn, we
collect, absorb knowledge,

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.730
learnings, meet people, and
then we exchange information,

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:20.290
and it's sort of like a master mind

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.390
when I'm just hanging out with my brother.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:26.641
And he'll fool you online,

00:09:26.641 --> 00:09:29.780
but I think, eventually
it'll be kind of understood,

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.030
Jake's a genius, the
kid is incredibly smart.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:34.440
And so I get to see that side of him,

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.210
which is always fascinating.

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:40.490
- Now I wanna ask like, an old
man question about civility.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:41.492
I'm gonna sound old for a second,

00:09:41.492 --> 00:09:43.200
but you know, it seems
like the thing that sticks

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.200
the most nowadays on social
media, is confrontation, anger,

00:09:49.976 --> 00:09:54.510
abrasiveness, hate, is
there room for love?

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.600
Is there room for being
somebody famous on the internet,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.320
if you're about positivity

00:10:00.638 --> 00:10:01.930
and using your forces for good?

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.350
I mean, it's clear there's been

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.040
a maturation in your process.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:06.030
That you're a different guy

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.580
than you were five, 10
years ago, for sure.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.420
- Yeah, it's a really great question.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:13.300
So my answer would,

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:15.640
and I'm a bit of an
idealist, would be yes.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:16.833
I hope so too.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:22.903
Unfortunately I think
most of our species loves

00:10:22.903 --> 00:10:26.140
the negativity, the drama,

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.913
just like that's kind of where we lean.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.680
For what reason, I don't know.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:35.830
And I've gotten the
opportunity to position

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:38.790
some really positive messaging
behind some of my content,

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:40.490
because of preconceived notions

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.113
about who I was. or who I am.

00:10:44.201 --> 00:10:45.440
Which is interesting.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.090
But yeah, you're absolutely right.

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.440
Spreading love, spreading
positivity, like in general,

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:51.190
like no one wants to see that shit.

00:10:52.822 --> 00:10:53.790
It's unfortunate and it's true,

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:54.893
and that's why the
people who are good at it

00:10:54.893 --> 00:10:57.000
are good at it,

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.050
and are making the waves that they are.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.250
Because when you can figure out

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:01.740
how to spread positive messaging,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.990
and really get people to feel and care,

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:07.044
which is kind of the staple of what I do.

00:11:07.044 --> 00:11:10.730
My only hope with my content,

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.846
